  "helpTitle": "Wake-on-LAN",
  "helpIntro": "Wake-on-LAN starts a sleeping computer when another device sends it a “magic packet” over the network. Ferret sends one when you tap Wake. The computer has to allow it first, once.",
  "helpToldTitle": "What {name} told Ferret",
  "@helpToldTitle": {
    "placeholders": {
      "name": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "helpThisComputer": "This computer",
  "cardWiredOn": "Cable · Wake-on-LAN is on",
  "cardWiredOff": "Cable · Wake-on-LAN is off",
  "cardWiredUnknown": "Cable · can't tell whether Wake-on-LAN is on",
  "cardWifi": "Wi-Fi · few computers wake over Wi-Fi",
  "cardNone": "No network card to wake it by yet. Open Ferret on it once.",
  "helpBestTitle": "What works",
  "helpBest": "A cable to the router, asleep rather than shut down, and another of your devices on the same network. Over Wi-Fi most computers don't wake at all: a cable makes it reliable.",
  "helpMacTitle": "Mac",
  "helpMac": "System Settings → Battery → Options → Wake for network access (on a desktop Mac: System Settings → Energy). A Mac on Wi-Fi wakes only through a Bonjour Sleep Proxy, such as an Apple TV or HomePod on the same network.",
  "helpWindowsTitle": "Windows",
  "helpWindows": "1. Device Manager → Network adapters → your Ethernet card → Properties.\n2. Power Management: turn on “Allow this device to wake the computer” and “Only allow a magic packet to wake the computer”.\n3. Advanced: set “Wake on Magic Packet” to Enabled.\n4. To wake it from shut down, turn off Fast startup: Control Panel → Power Options → Choose what the power buttons do.",
  "helpLinuxTitle": "Linux",
  "helpLinux": "Turn it on for the card: sudo ethtool -s enp3s0 wol g (use your card's name from “ip link”).\nKeep it after a restart: nmcli connection modify \"Wired connection 1\" 802-3-ethernet.wake-on-lan magic.",
  "helpFirmwareTitle": "BIOS or UEFI (Windows and Linux PCs)",
  "helpFirmware": "Press F2, Del or F12 while the PC starts. Turn on “Wake on LAN”, “Power On by PCI-E” or a setting like it, and turn off “ErP” or “Deep Sleep”, which cut power to the network card.",
  "helpIphoneTitle": "From an iPhone",
  "helpIphone": "iOS doesn't let apps broadcast on the network, so an iPhone can't send the magic packet by itself. Ferret asks another of your devices that is on, such as a Mac, a PC or ferret-cli on a home server, to send it for you.",
